  "implementation_notes": [
    "Each supported transport retains the original response object before the older typed SDK can drop audio/resource-link metadata, absent-vs-null fields, large resource sizes or unknown extension fields. Its internal carrier is overwritten on every raw response, consumed once by the adapter and never trusted as permission or host provenance.",
    "Canonical data.mcp_result retains every original result field; existing content/structured_content aliases remain. Host server/tool/success/telemetry are constructed independently, not merged from remote metadata.",
    "Mixed content and structuredContent remain together in model_result; top/block/resource protocol _meta stays in host data/DTOs. Explicit null gets a non-null model envelope so default_model_result cannot expose host metadata. Ordinary text-only results retain existing behavior.",
    "Supported block shapes are validated without the lossy old SDK conversion. Malformed/unknown content and malformed metadata produce explicit errors without payload-bearing diagnostics. Retention does not fetch resource URIs or render/execute metadata.",
    "Raw stdio framing uses the existing unbounded line semantics without the SDK malformed-frame payload debug log. Non-standard no-id notifications remain ignored; malformed standard messages and unsupported requests remain rejected. Closing drops the owned stdin pipe for EOF, retaining the existing three-second graceful wait and kill-on-drop ownership.",
    "The HTTP adapter retains accepted/no-content responses, authentication challenges, session headers and the existing configured reqwest client; GET/delete and SSE endpoint posting delegate to the same SDK client. Redirect and credential policies are unchanged.",
    "The real legacy declaration test consumes startup_timeout_sec/tool_timeout_sec at the production connection boundary and distinguishes transport timeout from an isError tool result.",
    "Existing media payload bytes are retained as protocol JSON/DTO data. Native provider image/audio presentation, UI rendering, public-service integration, subscriptions and other numbered capabilities are not asserted by task21."
  ],
